\f1\fs22 The Ablution occupied a narrow slice of real estate cantilevered off the underside of Profit Tower. To enter, you descended a spiral staircase that corkscrewed through the building\'92s \'91ground\'92 floor and into an glass capsule suspended inside the Layer. The establishment\'92s brochure described the experience as \'91bathing at the edge of eternity,\'92 which was technically accurate if eternity meant a roiling grey-brown soup of toxic particulate that killed everyone who fell into it.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Inside, a series of plunge pools were set into the transparent floor, each heated to temperatures calibrated by someone who believed comfort was a character flaw. The water was piped from high-altitude glacial reservoirs and infused with minerals whose names were longer than their health benefits. Bathers could look down at the Layer churning beneath them like a living thing with indigestion. The whole arrangement conveyed the serene confidence of the Airborne class: that even nakedness and vulnerability could be monetised, provided the poor were kept on the other side of the glass.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton was already in the hottest pool when Gerry arrived, arms spread along the rim, steam rising around him like a man being slowly rendered for stock. A towel attendant in clinical white guided Gerry to a robe and gestured toward the pool.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry looked down through the floor. The Layer shifted beneath his feet. He gripped the railing.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Get in, Gerry.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I\'92m fine here, CEO.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Nonsense. It\'92s therapeutic. The minerals are extraordinary. Something volcanic.\'92 Dunhilton smiled the way a crocodile smiles at a drinking hole.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry lowered himself in. The water was punishingly hot. Through the transparent floor beneath him, the Layer swirled and pulsed. He planted his feet firmly on the glass bottom and tried not to think about structural engineering.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton grew tense; he had a lot riding on the next few minutes. \'91Operation Parramatta,\'92 he said quietly and emphatically. \'91Status update please.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Your Serene Corporateness, we\'92ve recovered the virus.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton beamed, and exhaled with relief. \'91You mean the Parramatta Virus, yes?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Gerry tried to let it go, but he couldn\'92t. He had family living in Parramatta. \'91I wonder what the good citizens of Parramatta would think of naming a deadly virus after their fair city.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dunhilton looked puzzled. \'91
\f2\i You
\f1\i0  called it that. You said it was a Parramatta virus.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91I said it was a pararetrovirus.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91You don\'92t get much more retro than Parramatta.\'92 Dunhilton\'92s eyes narrowed. \'91You know, Gerry, I have your welfare at heart. I care about you.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, CEO.\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91And I want to keep caring about caring about you, Gerry. You understand?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Yes, CEO.\'92 Gerry found that all kinds of creepy. The water found new pores to scald.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>An awkward silence fell like a fog on a peat bog. \'91Well, what would 
\f2\i you
\f1\i0  call it?\'92\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'91Me, your Serene Corporateness?\'92 Gerry shrugged. \'91Perhaps, TMinusVirus?\'92\
