\'91We can\'92t have wealthy Cloudborne, especially not clones,\'92 Dunhilton blustered on. \'91It would be the end of civilised society as we know and live it, Gerry.\'92\
\'91Yes, CEO. When all is done and dusted, no one will cry over a few dead hospitality clones.\'92\
\'91Exactly right, Gerry. No one misses a broken dishwasher or espresso machine. They just throw it out and buy a new one, a better one. It\'92s good for the economy, Gerry, that\'92s what it is.\'92\
The steam rose between them. Below the glass, the Layer rolled over like a cat stretching in its sleep.\
\'91Indeed, your Serene Corporateness.\'92\
\'91So understand when I say, three months is too long to wait to clean the mice out of the grain silo.\'92 Dunhilton\'92s face of consternation transformed into that of a smiling Gaboon viper. \'91I\'92m depending on you, Gerry. You understand that\'85\'92\
\'91Have patience, CEO. I will have it ready to deploy come mid-November.\'92\
\'91I want it by Melbourne Cup Day, Gerry. No one will notice extra drug-related deaths for at least a week after that.\'92\
Gerry swallowed his objections. He knew if there weren\'92t Elvi dying by the Cadillac-load by the appointed time, he\'92d be dead instead. \'91The first Tuesday in November it is, your Serene Corporateness,\'92 he said, his neck so taut, he sounded like he might be being strangled.\
Dunhilton smiled and lowered himself until the water lapped at his chin. \'91Relax, Gerry. Enjoy the view.\'92\
Gerry looked down through the glass. The Layer stared back.
